const P=k=>PS[k];
/* [skill, passage, the question, the right choice, wrong choices, why] */
const ROWS=[
 ['diction','tw','According to this writer, what is at stake in choosing between two near-synonyms?','The gap between a nearly right word and the exact word can be large.',['Synonyms are interchangeable, so the choice hardly matters.','Only long words can change a sentence’s meaning.'],'The writer calls the difference a large matter. Word choice is not decoration: a nearly right word says something slightly different from the exact one.'],
 ['diction','thr','How do the words thrifty and cheap differ?','Same basic meaning, but thrifty has a positive connotation and cheap a negative one.',['They mean entirely different things, so neither carries a connotation.','Cheap is formal diction and thrifty is slang.'],'Both words denote someone careful with money. Their connotations, the feelings and judgments attached, differ: approval for thrifty, scorn for cheap.'],
 ['diction',null,'What is a word’s denotation?','Its literal dictionary meaning.',['The feelings and associations it carries.','The sound pattern of its syllables.'],'Denotation is the literal meaning. The feelings a word brings are its connotation.'],
 ['diction',null,'What is a word’s connotation?','The feelings and associations it carries beyond its literal meaning.',['Its exact definition in a dictionary.','The language it was borrowed from.'],'Connotation is the emotional and cultural color of a word. Home and house can name the same place, but home feels warmer.'],
 ['diction','form','Which level of diction does this sentence use?','Formal diction, with a stiff, official vocabulary',['Slang, as friends use in quick talk','Colloquial diction, as in casual speech'],'Requested, refrain and congregating are elaborate, official words. A casual writer would say Please do not hang out in the hall.'],
 ['diction','conc','What kind of diction fills this sentence?','Concrete diction, naming things the senses can detect',['Abstract diction, naming ideas such as justice','Jargon, terms known only to specialists'],'Cinnamon, toast, spoon and pot can be smelled, seen or heard. Concrete words make a scene vivid.'],
 ['diction','abs','What kind of diction does this sentence rely on?','Abstract diction, naming ideas and qualities',['Concrete diction, naming objects the senses can detect','Slang, as friends use in quick talk'],'Freedom, fairness and dignity are ideas, not things you can touch. Abstract words name big concepts and work best when backed by concrete examples.'],
 ['diction','jar','What effect might the jargon in this sentence have on a general reader?','It may confuse or distance readers who do not share the specialist vocabulary.',['It makes the sentence vivid and concrete.','It signals warm, casual friendship.'],'Operationalize, stakeholder-aligned and verticals belong to a field. Outside that field they can sound vague or cold.'],
 ['diction','loaded','Which pair of words carries the strongest negative connotation?','scheme and squander',['mayor’s and town’s','will and money'],'A plan becomes a scheme when the writer distrusts it, and spend becomes squander when the writer disapproves. Both words pass judgment.'],
 ['diction','walk','The writer wants the visitors to seem relaxed. Which verb best fills the blank?','strolled',['stomped','charged'],'Strolled suggests an easy, unhurried walk. Stomped and charged suggest anger or haste, which would clash with pausing to watch gulls.'],
 ['diction','vague','Which revision is the most precise?','The bakery was warm and fragrant, with trays of rolls cooling on every shelf.',['The bakery was really very nice.','The bakery was good in many ways.'],'The right choice swaps the vague word nice for specific, sensory detail. Really very nice and good in many ways stay just as vague.'],
 ['diction',null,'Why does a writer’s diction usually shift between a text to a friend and a letter to a city council?','Word choice is matched to the audience and the occasion.',['Diction is fixed and cannot change.','Formal words are always more persuasive.'],'Style fits the rhetorical situation. A casual word can charm a friend and undercut a council letter.'],
 ['diction','crowd','What does the choice of crowd over mob do?','It gives a more positive, orderly impression of the protesters.',['It makes the protesters sound violent.','It has no effect, since the words are exact synonyms.'],'Mob connotes disorder and threat; crowd is neutral. The writer’s denial of mob steers readers toward a gentler view.'],
 ['diction','rights','What is rightsizing in this sentence?','A euphemism that softens the idea of laying off workers',['A hyperbole that exaggerates how many workers there are','A simile that compares workers to clothes'],'A euphemism is a mild word for an unpleasant idea. Rightsizing sounds technical and calm, while laying off sounds harsh.'],
 ['diction','smug','Which word most strongly shows the narrator’s disapproval of the woman?','smug',['hall','waited'],'Smug means pleased with oneself in an irritating way, so it judges her. Hall and waited merely report.'],
 ['diction','river','What do the three verbs have in common?','They share a snakelike motion that creates an uneasy mood.',['They are all abstract nouns.','They are all formal legal terms.'],'Crept, coiled and hissed all suit a snake. The pattern of diction gives the river a menacing feel.'],
 ['diction',null,'A writer wants to praise a child who asks many questions. Which word is the best choice?','curious',['nosy','prying'],'Curious has a positive connotation. Nosy and prying suggest unwelcome snooping.'],
 ['diction',null,'Why might a writer choose a short, plain word such as end over termination?','Plain diction can sound direct and sincere.',['Short words are always incorrect in serious writing.','Longer words are always clearer.'],'Writers choose plain words when they want directness. Longer words are not automatically clearer or better.'],
 ['compare',null,'What marks a simile?','A comparison that uses like or as.',['A comparison that says one thing is another.','A statement that exaggerates for effect.'],'A simile signals the comparison with like or as. A metaphor states it outright.'],
 ['compare',null,'In figurative language, what is a metaphor?','A comparison that says one thing is another, without like or as.',['A comparison that always uses like or as.','A line repeated at the start of sentences.'],'A metaphor equates two unlike things, such as calling a library a treasure chest.'],
 ['compare','bus','Which figure of speech does this sentence use?','A simile, a comparison with like',['A metaphor, a comparison without like or as','Personification, giving ideas to an object only'],'The bus is compared to a tired smoker with the word like, so it is a simile. The cough also hints at personification.'],
 ['compare','thief','Which figure of speech is this?','A metaphor, which equates time and a thief',['A simile, which uses like or as','Understatement, which says less than is meant'],'Time is called a thief without like or as. The metaphor suggests that time steals things quietly.'],
 ['compare','wind','Which figure of speech does the writer use?','Personification, giving the wind a human act',['Simile, using like or as','Oxymoron, joining opposite words'],'Wind cannot mutter or complain. The writer gives it human behavior, which is personification.'],
 ['compare','compass','What does the writer explain by analogy?','A thesis, explained by likening it to a compass',['A compass, explained by likening it to a thesis','A trip, explained by listing its dates'],'An analogy explains something unfamiliar or abstract by likening it to something familiar. Here the thesis is explained through a compass.'],
 ['compare',null,'What is the main job of an analogy in an argument?','To explain or support an idea by likening it to something familiar.',['To replace all evidence with a picture.','To list every fact about a topic.'],'Analogies make a hard idea easier to grasp, and can support a claim when the two things really are alike.'],
 ['compare','ship','What is this device?','An extended metaphor, developed over several sentences',['A single simile using like','A rhetorical question'],'The ship metaphor is carried through steers, sails and crew. A metaphor developed this way is called extended.'],
 ['compare','ship','What do the ropes we did not choose suggest about the students?','They have little say in the work they are given.',['They are in charge of the whole ship.','They enjoy every task.'],'The students haul ropes someone else chose, so the metaphor suggests tasks without choice.'],
 ['compare','tw','In this sentence, what does the lightning-bug stand for?','The almost right word',['The right word','The writer’s audience'],'The sentence sets the almost right word against the right word and then likens that gap to the one between the lightning-bug and the lightning.'],
 ['compare','tw','Why does the writer bring in the lightning-bug and the lightning?','A familiar comparison shows how big the gap is between a near word and the exact word.',['It lists two kinds of insects for a science lesson.','It proves that all words mean the same.'],'The gap between a faint glow and a bolt is huge. The comparison makes a small-sounding difference feel large.'],
 ['compare','cards','What is wrong with the comparison in this sentence?','It mixes unrelated images (cards, sinking, taking off).',['Nothing: mixing images always strengthens a sentence.','It is a literal statement with no comparison.'],'A house of cards collapses; it does not sink or take off. Combining clashing images is a mixed metaphor.'],
 ['compare','sun','Why might a reader find this comparison weak?','It is a cliché, a stale comparison readers have met many times.',['It is too original to understand.','It is not a comparison at all.'],'Bright as the sun is a worn-out comparison. A fresh, specific image would show more.'],
 ['compare',null,'Which sentence makes the freshest, most specific comparison?','The silent library was a held breath.',['The library was as quiet as a mouse.','The library was very, very quiet.'],'A held breath is a surprising, exact image of tension in the quiet. As quiet as a mouse is a cliché, and very, very quiet makes no comparison.'],
 ['compare',null,'How does an analogy usually differ from a simple simile?','It explains a point by drawing out several parallels between two things.',['It never uses figurative language.','It always contains exactly one word.'],'A simile may only notice one likeness. An analogy follows the likeness far enough to explain or argue something.'],
 ['compare','scal','What does the metaphor suggest about her argument?','It is precise rather than forceful.',['It is careless and loud.','It is very long.'],'A scalpel cuts exactly and a hammer pounds. The metaphor praises precision.'],
 ['compare','car','What is the best response from a critic of this analogy?','Students are not machines, so the analogy may ignore differences that matter.',['Cars are expensive, so the analogy fails.','The analogy proves the claim because it is vivid.'],'An analogy supports a claim only if the two things are alike in ways that matter. A critic tests that, not how vivid the picture is.'],
 ['compare',null,'Which sentence is figurative rather than literal?','My backpack weighs a ton.',['My backpack weighs six pounds.','My backpack is blue.'],'A backpack cannot weigh a ton, so the line exaggerates for effect. The others state facts.'],
 ['syntax','shortp','What effect do the short sentences create?','Tension and urgency',['A calm, flowing description','A long list of facts'],'Brief, blunt sentences stop and start the reader, which suits waiting for a storm.'],
 ['syntax','longp','What effect does the single long sentence have?','The sentence unrolls like the walk itself, without a pause.',['It sounds abrupt and choppy.','It shows that the narrator is angry.'],'Long sentences with many and phrases carry the reader along. The length mirrors the length of the walk.'],
 ['syntax','avg','What is the average sentence length in words?','4 words',['12 words','6 words'],'The sentences have 2, 4 and 6 words. Their total is 12 and 12 ÷ 3 = 4. The wrong choices give the total and the longest sentence.'],
 ['syntax',null,'What is a periodic sentence?','One that holds its main clause until the end.',['One that states its main clause first and then adds details.','One that has exactly one word.'],'A periodic sentence builds with modifiers and delays the main clause until the end.'],
 ['syntax',null,'What is a loose, or cumulative, sentence?','One that states its main clause first, then adds details.',['One that delays its main clause until the end.','One made of fragments only.'],'The main clause comes early and the details trail after it.'],
 ['syntax','perio','Is this sentence periodic or loose?','Periodic, since the main clause comes last',['Loose, since the main clause comes first','Neither, since it has no clauses'],'Three opening phrases pile up before Maya finally opened her letter, the main clause, arrives at the end.'],
 ['syntax','loose','Is this sentence periodic or loose?','Loose, since the main clause comes first and details follow',['Periodic, since the main clause is held until the end','Neither, since it has no main clause'],'Maya opened her letter at last is complete on its own. The shaking hands, the dark hall and the gone buses are added after it.'],
 ['syntax',null,'What effect does a periodic sentence often have on a reader?','It builds suspense, because the point waits until the end.',['It reveals the point at once and then repeats it.','It makes the sentence shorter.'],'Holding back the main clause makes the reader wait, then rewards the wait with the point.'],
 ['syntax','bu','How does the short first sentence differ from the second?','It delivers a blunt, final statement before a longer sentence develops it.',['It is longer and more detailed than the second.','It asks a question the second answers.'],'The first is brief, with a flat declarative finality. The second is longer and lists what replaced chivalry.'],
 ['syntax','bu','The second sentence names sophisters, economists, and calculators. What does this list of three do?','It builds a rhythm that gives the group weight and a scornful tone.',['It provides statistics.','It makes the sentence a question.'],'Writers often use three items for rhythm and completeness. Here the list also puts three disliked types of thinker side by side.'],
 ['syntax','inv','What is unusual about this sentence’s word order?','The usual subject-verb order is inverted, which stresses the opening phrase.',['It has no verb.','It is written as a question.'],'Normal order would be The whale swam into the dark. Putting into the dark first and the whale last creates a more dramatic, delayed image.'],
 ['syntax','frag','Why might a writer use the fragment Finally?','For emphasis, imitating the sound of a sigh or thought.',['Fragments are required in formal writing.','Because the writer forgot the verb.'],'An intentional fragment can stress a feeling. It works best when readers can see it is deliberate.'],
 ['syntax','sub','Which idea sits in the main clause, and so gets the main emphasis?','it failed',['the plan was cheap','Although'],'Although begins a dependent clause. The main clause is it failed, which carries the point.'],
 ['syntax','poly','What device is the repeated and in this sentence?','Polysyndeton, repeated conjunctions that slow the pace',['Asyndeton, omitted conjunctions that quicken the pace','Antithesis, a contrast of opposites'],'Linking every verb with and makes the list feel long and tiring, like the work itself.'],
 ['syntax','asyn','What device leaves out the word and from this list?','Asyndeton, which omits conjunctions and speeds the pace',['Polysyndeton, which repeats conjunctions','Personification, which gives things human traits'],'Without conjunctions the verbs follow each other fast and with a hammering rhythm.'],
 ['syntax','count','How many words are in the longest sentence?','5 words',['3 words','7 words'],'The sentences have 1, 1 and 5 words. Count to ten, then answer is the longest. Seven is the total of all the words.'],
 ['syntax',null,'Why do skilled writers vary the length of their sentences?','Variety controls pace and emphasis and avoids a monotonous rhythm.',['Rules require every third sentence to be short.','Longer sentences are always better.'],'A mix of lengths lets a writer slow down for detail and speed up for punch.'],
 ['syntax','imper','What kind of sentence is this, and what does it do?','Imperative, giving a command',['Interrogative, asking a question','Exclamatory, showing strong feeling only'],'It has an understood you and tells the reader to act. Imperatives can sound firm or direct.'],
 ['devices',null,'What is parallelism?','Using the same grammatical form for related ideas.',['Using words that sound like their meaning.','Placing the strongest idea first only.'],'Parallel structure repeats a form, so related ideas look and sound alike.'],
 ['devices','anti','What device sets pity against fairness?','Antithesis, which contrasts opposed ideas in balanced form',['Anaphora, which repeats words at the start of clauses','Simile, which uses like or as'],'Not for pity but for fairness places two opposed ideas in a balanced frame, which is antithesis.'],
 ['devices','anaph','What device repeats We will at the start of each sentence?','Anaphora, repetition at the start of clauses',['Epistrophe, repetition at the end of clauses','Asyndeton, leaving out conjunctions'],'Beginning each sentence with the same words is anaphora.'],
 ['devices','anaph','What is one effect of this repetition?','It stresses each promise and builds a steady, determined rhythm.',['It hides what the speaker will do.','It turns the promises into questions.'],'Repeating the opening makes each task stand out and gives the passage a marching beat.'],
 ['devices','rq','What does this rhetorical question do?','It asks a question that needs no answer, so readers reach the writer’s conclusion themselves.',['It asks for information the writer lacks.','It states a fact with a number.'],'A rhetorical question is asked for effect. The expected answer is obvious: too long.'],
 ['devices',null,'Which sentence uses parallel structure?','The coach asked us to run, to stretch and to listen.',['The coach asked us to run, stretching, and that we listen.','The coach asked us to run, to stretch and for listening.'],'Each item in the right choice is to plus a verb. The others switch forms midway.'],
 ['devices','epis','What is the repetition of helped at the ends of the clauses called?','Epistrophe',['Anaphora','Antithesis'],'Epistrophe repeats a word at the ends of clauses. The repeated When we were also forms anaphora at the starts.'],
 ['devices','anti','What does the structure of the antithesis do for the speaker’s claim?','It sharpens the claim by setting what is rejected beside what is wanted.',['It weakens the claim by listing doubts.','It makes the sentence a command.'],'Placing pity beside fairness makes the speaker’s real demand stand out against what is refused.'],
 ['devices','mission','What makes this list parallel?','Each item begins with to and a verb, followed by an adverb.',['Every item is a noun.','Each item is a full sentence.'],'To read widely, to write clearly and to argue fairly all have the same shape.'],
 ['devices',null,'Which is a rhetorical question, not a genuine request for information?','Do we really want to be the town that let its library close?',['What time does the library close?','Can you tell me where the library is?'],'The first expects no reply and pushes the audience toward no. The others ask for facts.'],
 ['devices','wo','What contrast sets the two halves of the sentence against each other?','Power over men versus power over themselves',['Men versus the government','Women versus nature'],'The writer narrows her request: not power over men but over themselves. The contrast is the point of the sentence.'],
 ['devices','wo','How does the structure of this sentence, with its semicolon and but, serve the writer?','It balances two parallel phrases so the narrowed claim stands out.',['It lists unrelated examples.','It turns the claim into a question.'],'Over men and over themselves have the same shape, and but turns from one to the other. Balance gives the narrowed claim its force.'],
 ['devices',null,'Which sentence uses antithesis?','The plan is quick to start but slow to finish.',['The plan starts in May and ends in June.','The plan is long, and it is detailed.'],'Quick to start and slow to finish are opposed ideas in balanced form.'],
 ['devices',null,'What risk comes from using a device such as anaphora in every sentence?','It can feel mechanical and draw attention away from the message.',['It always makes an argument stronger.','It makes readers skip to the end.'],'Devices work by standing out. Overused, they become a pattern the reader notices more than the point.'],
 ['devices','learn','How many parallel phrases does this sentence contain?','Three',['Two','Four'],'To learn, to build and to lead are three parallel infinitive phrases.'],
 ['devices','every','How many sentences in a row begin with the word Every?','Three',['Two','Four'],'Every door, Every window and Every light begin the first three sentences. The fourth begins with Still.'],
 ['devices','plan','Which pair of phrases forms the antithesis?','small in cost / large in promise',['The plan / is small','but large / the plan'],'The pair small in cost and large in promise set opposite ideas in parallel form.'],
 ['devices','chi','What is the reversed pattern in this sentence called?','Chiasmus, a reversal of word order in the second half',['Anaphora, repetition at the start of clauses','Simile, a comparison with like'],'Live to work becomes work to live. Reversing the order of the same words is chiasmus.'],
 ['tone',null,'In a piece of writing, what is meant by tone?','The writer’s attitude toward the subject or the audience.',['The loudness of the narrator’s voice.','The number of syllables in a line.'],'Tone is the attitude a writer conveys, such as admiring, scornful or hopeful.'],
 ['tone',null,'How does tone differ from mood?','Tone is the writer’s attitude; mood is the feeling created in the reader.',['Tone is the setting; mood is the plot.','They are two names for the same thing.'],'A writer’s tone may be playful while the mood of a scene feels eerie. They are related but not identical.'],
 ['tone','nost','What is the tone of this passage?','Nostalgic, fondly longing for the past',['Indignant, angry at an injustice','Detached, reporting facts without feeling'],'The sound of the screen door and the smell of pies, along with the wish to return, show fond longing for the past.'],
 ['tone','indig','What is the tone of this passage?','Indignant, angry at an injustice',['Nostalgic, fondly longing for the past','Playful, joking with the reader'],'Three winters of neglect and the closing outrage show anger at unfair treatment.'],
 ['tone','iron','What tone does this sentence use?','Sarcastic or ironic, saying the opposite of what is meant',['Sincerely delighted','Neutral and informative'],'No one enjoys a ninety-minute wait for a ninety-second form. The praise is meant as complaint.'],
 ['tone','obj','What tone does this sentence have?','Objective and matter-of-fact',['Angry and accusing','Wistful and dreamy'],'It gives hours without any judgment or feeling. That plain reporting is an objective tone.'],
 ['tone','hope','What tone does this passage convey?','Hopeful and optimistic',['Bitter and resentful','Bored and indifferent'],'Small promise and spring keeps its promises both look forward with confidence.'],
 ['tone','solemn','What tone does this sentence convey?','Solemn and respectful',['Joking and playful','Hostile and mocking'],'Gathering in silence to honor others is a respectful, serious act.'],
 ['tone','cond','What tone does the speaker take toward young people?','Condescending, talking down to them',['Admiring, praising their skill','Neutral, only reporting a fact'],'Of course and cannot be expected suggest the speaker thinks young people are not capable.'],
 ['tone','humor','What tone does the locker comparison create?','Humorous and playful through exaggeration',['Solemn and prayerful','Furious and accusing'],'Calling a messy locker an archaeological dig is a joking exaggeration.'],
 ['tone','urg','What tone does this passage convey?','Urgent, pressing the audience to act at once',['Relaxed, in no hurry at all','Nostalgic, missing the past'],'Short commands and the warning about the levee press for immediate action.'],
 ['tone',null,'Which elements work together to create a writer’s tone?','Diction, syntax, imagery and details together.',['Only the title of the work.','Only the length of the paragraphs.'],'Tone comes from many choices at once: the words chosen, the sentence shapes, the images and the details.'],
 ['tone','shift','What shift in tone does this passage show?','A move from cheerful to grim or sorrowful',['A move from sorrowful to cheerful','No change at all'],'The first sentence is bright and sweet. After the letter, nothing was sweet again turns the tone dark.'],
 ['tone','iron','Which word most clearly signals the sarcasm?','delight, which contradicts the situation described',['ninety, which is a number','form, which is a noun'],'A ninety-minute wait is not a delight. The clash between the word and the situation exposes the irony.'],
 ['tone','bu','As one reading, what tone does the last clause suggest?','Mournful regret for something lost',['Cheerful welcome of change','Cool indifference'],'Extinguished and forever treat the loss as complete and final, which reads as sorrow for what has gone.'],
 ['tone','tw','As one reading, which tone best describes the comparison to the lightning-bug?','Playful, yet serious about the craft of choosing words',['Bitter and hopeless','Cold and purely factual'],'The image of an insect and a bolt is witty, but it makes a serious point about precision.'],
 ['tone',null,'Which pair names two nearly opposite tones?','reverent and mocking',['wistful and nostalgic','urgent and pressing'],'Reverent shows deep respect while mocking shows scorn. The other pairs are close in meaning.'],
 ['tone','firm','What tone do the short, repeated refusals create?','Firm and defiant',['Uncertain and hesitant','Dreamy and relaxed'],'No, repeated and followed by not today, not ever, shuts the door on discussion.'],
 ['purpose',null,'How can a writer’s style help build ethos, or credibility?','Precise, fair and controlled language shows competence and good faith.',['Using as many long words as possible.','Avoiding all evidence and relying on volume.'],'Readers trust writers who sound careful, fair and in command of their subject.'],
 ['purpose','lab','How might the style affect the board’s view of her?','The board may doubt her seriousness, since the style does not fit the context.',['The board will find her more credible for sounding casual.','The style will have no possible effect.'],'Style should fit the occasion. Slang and exclamation points can make a scientific report seem careless.'],
 ['purpose',null,'A writer’s purpose is to calm worried parents. Which style serves it best?','Calm, plain, reassuring diction and steady sentences.',['Dramatic warnings in short, panicked bursts.','Heavy jargon that only specialists understand.'],'A calming purpose is served by a steady voice and clear words. Alarmed or confusing language would work against it.'],
 ['purpose',null,'A writer’s purpose is to explain how to set up a tent. Which style choice fits?','Clear, concrete diction and simple sentences in order.',['Long periodic sentences full of abstract nouns.','Humorous exaggeration in every line.'],'Instructions need clarity and sequence. Concrete words and simple sentences help the reader follow.'],
 ['purpose',null,'Which sentence best explains how a style choice serves a purpose?','The writer’s short, firm sentences press the audience to act now.',['The writer uses short sentences.','The sentences are short and then long.'],'The best answer names the choice, the effect and the purpose. The others only describe the choice.'],
 ['purpose','fools','How might this style affect credibility with undecided readers?','The insult may weaken credibility, since it attacks people instead of giving reasons.',['It strengthens credibility, since it is forceful.','It has no effect on readers’ trust.'],'Name-calling suggests there are no good reasons. Undecided readers may trust the writer less.'],
 ['purpose','hey','Which revision best suits a formal letter to the principal?','The gym roof needs urgent repair.',['The gym roof is super broken, honestly.','The gym roof, like, leaks.'],'A letter to a principal calls for formal, direct diction. The other choices stay casual.'],
 ['purpose','wordy','Which revision is the most concise without losing meaning?','Because it rained, the game was postponed.',['Due to the fact of rain, there was a postponement of the game.','The game, owing to rain, had a postponement.'],'Because says what Due to the fact that says in far fewer words. Concise style respects the reader’s time.'],
 ['purpose','passv','Why might We made mistakes build more credibility than Mistakes were made?','It names the actor, so the writer seems to take responsibility.',['It is longer, and long sentences are always more honest.','It uses the passive voice, which is always better.'],'Passive voice can hide who acted. Naming the actor can read as accountable, though passive voice has other legitimate uses.'],
 ['purpose',null,'A writer addresses seven-year-olds. Which style choice fits the audience?','Short sentences and concrete, familiar words',['Dense jargon and long periodic sentences','Abstract nouns with no examples'],'Style should fit the audience. Young readers do better with concrete words and short sentences.'],
 ['purpose',null,'A student wants to end a paragraph on a striking note. Which choice could help?','A short, vivid sentence after several longer ones',['A fifth long sentence like the others','An unrelated new topic'],'A short sentence after longer ones stands out. That contrast in length gives the ending weight.'],
 ['purpose','notc','What does this contrast do for the speaker’s image?','It presents the group as constructive rather than complaining.',['It shows the group has no plan.','It makes the group seem uninterested.'],'Did not come to complain and came to build sets constructive purpose against whining, which flatters the speaker.'],
 ['purpose',null,'Which statement about style is accurate?','Style is a set of choices that can be analyzed for how it serves a purpose.',['Style is only decoration that adds nothing to meaning.','Style matters only in poetry.'],'Diction, syntax and tone all shape how a message lands, so analysts ask what each choice does.'],
 ['purpose',null,'Why might a writer who chooses the exact word seem credible?','Precision signals care and command of the subject.',['Readers trust every long word.','Exact words always make writing longer.'],'A writer who picks the right word seems to know the material and to respect the reader.'],
 ['purpose','wo','By asking only for power over themselves, how does the writer shape her credibility?','She makes a measured, defensible claim rather than a sweeping one.',['She admits that she has no argument.','She asks for power over everyone.'],'Narrowing the claim makes it easier for a skeptical reader to accept. Measured demands often read as more trustworthy.'],
 ['purpose','hype','Which revision is the most measured and credible?','This plan is the strongest of the three options.',['This plan is absolutely the greatest idea in history!!!','This plan is okay, I guess, maybe.'],'The right choice is firm but specific and can be backed with evidence. The others either exaggerate or hedge until the claim is empty.'],
 ['purpose','we','What effect does the repeated our and we have?','It builds a sense of shared identity with the audience.',['It makes the speech more formal.','It separates the speaker from the audience.'],'Inclusive pronouns invite the audience into the work, which can build goodwill.']];
const TRY=['Fictional passage: “The detective called the witness’s story a fairy tale, while the lawyer called it an honest memory.” The two describe the same story. What does the difference in word choice show? A: Both speakers share the same attitude, B: The detective doubts the story and the lawyer trusts it, C: The story is literally about fairies. Answer A, B or C.',
 'Fictional passage: “The old system was slow to listen and quick to blame.” Which device does the sentence use? A: Anaphora, B: Personification, C: Antithesis. Answer A, B or C.',
 'Fictional passage: “Oh, wonderful, another meeting to plan the meeting about the meeting.” What is the tone? A: Sarcastic, B: Solemn, C: Hopeful. Answer A, B or C.'];
const tryIt=s=>`Try it: ${s[0].toLowerCase()+s.slice(1)}`;
export default {
id:'language-h-ap-lang-style',subject:'language',level:5,grade:9,standard:'AP English Language and Composition Course and Exam Description (College Board), Unit 4: Big Idea STL (Style)',
title:'Style',
goal:'Read and write with the choices of style in view: weigh word choice, connotation and levels of diction; read similes, metaphors and analogies; see how sentence length and structure shape pace; recognize parallelism, antithesis, repetition and rhetorical questions; name a writer’s tone and the evidence for it; and explain how style serves a purpose and builds credibility.',
points:20,
skills:{diction:'Word choice',compare:'Comparisons: simile, metaphor, analogy',syntax:'Sentence structure',devices:'Parallelism, antithesis and repetition',tone:'Tone',purpose:'Style, purpose and credibility'},
outline:[['STL (word choice)','Explain how denotation, connotation and levels of diction contribute to a writer’s purpose',['diction']],['STL (comparisons)','Explain how similes, metaphors and analogies make or support a point',['compare']],['STL (sentence structure)','Explain how sentence length, structure and order shape emphasis and pace',['syntax']],['STL (parallelism, antithesis and repetition)','Explain how parallelism, antithesis, repetition and rhetorical questions create effects',['devices']],['STL (tone)','Identify a writer’s tone and the choices that create it',['tone']],['STL (style, purpose and credibility)','Explain how style choices serve a purpose and build credibility, and make intentional style choices in writing',['purpose']]],
steps:[
 {skill:'diction',say:'Style is the sum of a writer’s choices about words, sentences and attitude, and each choice should serve a purpose for an audience. Three kinds of choice carry most of it: diction, or word choice; syntax, or how sentences are built; and tone, or the writer’s attitude. Reading for style means asking what each choice does, not just what it is called.',pic:flow('Style: choices that serve a purpose',[['Words','Diction'],['Sentences','Syntax'],['Attitude','Tone']])},
 {skill:'diction',say:'Every word has a denotation, its literal meaning, and a connotation, the feelings and associations it carries. Slim, skinny and scrawny can all describe a thin person, yet slim sounds admiring and scrawny sounds unkind. A writer who wants a reader to approve or disapprove picks words by connotation as well as by meaning.',pic:board('Denotation and connotation',['Denotation: thin, not heavy','Connotation of slim: admiring','Connotation of scrawny: unkind','Same denotation, different judgment'],['the dictionary meaning','a positive tone','a negative tone','word choice steers the reader'])},
 {skill:'diction',say:'Diction also has levels and kinds. Formal diction sounds official, colloquial diction sounds like everyday talk, and slang is very casual, often short-lived, wording. Concrete words name things the senses can detect, while abstract words name ideas such as honor or fairness. Jargon is vocabulary known to a specialist group. Good writers match the level and kind to their audience.',pic:grid('Kinds of diction',['Formal','Colloquial','Slang','Concrete','Abstract','Jargon']),
  check:{prompt:tryIt(TRY[0]),answer:'B',options:['A','B','C'],hint:'Fairy tale suggests something invented; honest memory suggests something true. Whose attitude does each phrase reveal?',pic:pass('Fictional passage',['The detective called the witness’s story a fairy tale, while the lawyer called it an honest memory.'])}},
 {skill:'compare',say:'Writers explain and persuade by comparison. A simile uses like or as to compare two unlike things, while a metaphor says one thing is another. Personification gives human actions to something not human, and hyperbole exaggerates on purpose. An analogy goes further: it explains an unfamiliar idea by drawing out the points it shares with a familiar one.',pic:grid('Figures of comparison',['Simile','Metaphor','Personification','Hyperbole','Analogy','Extended metaphor'])},
 {skill:'compare',say:'Test a comparison by asking what it makes the reader notice. In the fictional analogy shown, a budget is likened to a leaky bucket, which makes the reader think about where water escapes. A good comparison is specific, fits the point, and does not mix clashing images. A stale cliché shows less than a fresh image would.',pic:board('Testing a comparison',['A budget is a leaky bucket','It points readers to where money escapes','Check: do the two things match in ways that matter?','Avoid clichés and mixed images'],['the comparison','what it shows','the fair test','keep it fresh'])},
 {skill:'syntax',say:'Syntax is the arrangement of words into sentences, and its first lever is length. Short sentences hit quickly and can create tension or force, while long sentences can carry the reader through a scene or a thought. In the fictional paragraph charted here the sentences run 3, 3 and 12 words, so the average is (3 + 3 + 12) ÷ 3 = 6, and the long one stands out because of the short ones.',pic:bars('Fictional: words in each sentence',[['Sentence 1',3],['Sentence 2',3],['Sentence 3',12]])},
 {skill:'syntax',say:'Sentence structure shapes emphasis too. A loose sentence states its main clause first and adds details after it, so the point comes early. A periodic sentence holds the main clause until the end, building suspense. Inverted word order, a deliberate fragment, or a string of and links can also change pace and draw attention to a word.',pic:board('Loose or periodic?',['Loose: main clause, then details','Periodic: details, then main clause','Fictional loose: She won, tired and proud.','Fictional periodic: Tired and proud, she won.'],['the point comes early','the point is held back','the main clause is first','the main clause is last'])},
 {skill:'syntax',say:'Syntax carries tone as well as pace. The sentences of Edmund Burke from 1790 pair a very short statement with a longer one that lists what replaced it, and the contrast gives the first sentence a final sound. When you analyze a sentence, name its length, its order and its structure, then say what that does to the reader’s attention.',pic:pass('Fictional: short, then long',['It ended. The old clock tower, the market square and the long summer evenings it had watched over all went quiet together.'])},
 {skill:'devices',say:'Some devices work by repeating or balancing a form. Parallelism gives related ideas the same grammatical shape. Antithesis sets opposed ideas side by side in a balanced frame. Anaphora repeats words at the start of clauses and epistrophe repeats them at the ends. Chiasmus reverses a pattern, and a rhetorical question is asked for effect, not for an answer.',pic:grid('Devices of balance and repetition',['Parallelism','Antithesis','Anaphora','Epistrophe','Chiasmus','Rhetorical question']),
  check:{prompt:tryIt(TRY[1]),answer:'C',options:['A','B','C'],hint:'Look at the two halves, slow to listen and quick to blame. Are the ideas alike or opposed?',pic:pass('Fictional passage',['The old system was slow to listen and quick to blame.'])}},
 {skill:'devices',say:'Name the device, then say what it does. In the fictional line shown, the speaker says not a gift but a right, and the antithesis shrinks one idea to enlarge the other. Anaphora would build momentum, and parallelism would make a list easy to remember. A student who writes only that a device is used has not yet analyzed it; the effect on the audience is the point.',pic:board('Device, then effect',['Fictional: This is not a gift but a right.','Device: antithesis, not a gift but a right','Effect: the claim is raised above charity','Always add: what it does for the purpose'],['the sentence','the name','the effect','the analysis'])},
 {skill:'devices',say:'A real writer shows the same moves. Mary Wollstonecraft, writing in 1792, narrows her demand to power over themselves rather than over men, with two balanced phrases set against each other. The balance makes a measured claim sound reasonable, not threatening. Notice that the form, one phrase answering another, and the content, a limited claim, work together.',pic:pass('Fictional: balanced phrases, a limited claim',['We seek a voice in the plan, not a veto over it.'])},
 {skill:'devices',say:'Rhetorical questions invite the audience to supply the obvious answer, which can feel like the audience’s own conclusion. Devices can also be overused: a speech built from anaphora in every sentence may sound like a chant. Use a device when it serves the purpose, and vary your sentences so the strongest moment still stands out.',pic:board('Using devices well',['A rhetorical question leads the reader to the answer','Anaphora builds momentum','Overuse makes the pattern louder than the point','Use each device for a reason'],['readers reply','one pattern','a chant','purpose first'])},
 {skill:'tone',say:'Tone is the writer’s attitude toward the subject or the audience, such as admiring, scornful, wistful, urgent, ironic or matter-of-fact. Mood is different: it is the feeling a passage creates in the reader. To name a tone, collect evidence from diction, syntax, imagery and detail, and then choose a precise word instead of just saying positive or negative.',pic:grid('A few tones',['Nostalgic','Indignant','Ironic','Objective','Hopeful','Condescending']),
  check:{prompt:tryIt(TRY[2]),answer:'A',options:['A','B','C'],hint:'The speaker calls a pointless meeting wonderful. Does the praise match the situation?',pic:pass('Fictional passage',['Oh, wonderful, another meeting to plan the meeting about the meeting.'])}},
 {skill:'tone',say:'Tone can shift inside a passage. A cheerful opening followed by the word but, or by a sudden short sentence, often signals a turn. Irony is a special case: the words say one thing while the situation shows another, so praise can really be complaint. Watch for the shift and quote the words that signal it, since one word often carries the turn.',pic:flow('A tone shift',[['Cheerful','Bright details'],['Turn','But, then, suddenly'],['Grim','Dark details']])},
 {skill:'purpose',say:'Style serves purpose and audience. A writer who wants to calm readers chooses a steady voice and plain words; a writer who wants action may use short, firm sentences and repeated commands. To analyze a choice, name it, say what it does, and link it to the writer’s purpose. The last step is the one many students leave out.',pic:board('From choice to purpose',['Choice: short, firm sentences','Effect: the pace feels urgent','Purpose: to move the audience to act','Link all three in one sentence'],['name it','say what it does','tie it to the aim','a full analysis'])},
 {skill:'purpose',say:'Style also builds credibility, which is ethos. Precise word choice, fair treatment of other views and a measured tone show a writer is careful and honest, while insults, slang in a formal setting and wild exaggeration can lose readers. In your own writing, revise with these questions: is the word exact, is the sentence the right length, and does the tone fit the audience?',pic:board('Style and credibility',['Precise words show care','Fair, measured claims invite trust','Insults and hype lose undecided readers','Revise for audience and purpose'],['exact diction','a measured tone','what to avoid','the final check'])},
 {skill:'purpose',summary:true,say:'Tie the unit together with one habit. For any passage, ask what the word choice, the sentence shapes, the devices and the tone each do, then connect them to the writer’s purpose and audience. Avoid the common mistakes: naming a device without its effect, calling every tone positive or negative, and forgetting that style choices are chosen on purpose. In your own writing, make those choices on purpose too.',pic:flow('Style: the habit',[['Notice','Words, sentences, devices'],['Explain','What each does'],['Connect','Purpose and audience']])}],
progression:{requires:['language-h-ap-lang-reasoning-and-organization'],operations:2,demand:'Read and write with the choices of style in view: weigh word choice and connotation, read comparisons, see how sentence structure shapes pace and emphasis, recognize parallelism, antithesis and repetition, name a writer’s tone and the evidence for it, and explain how style serves a purpose and builds credibility.'},
items(add){
 const seen=new Set(TRY);
 const put=(skill,prompt,answer,pic,explanation,accept)=>{if(seen.has(prompt))return;seen.add(prompt);add(skill,prompt,answer,pic,explanation,accept);};
 ROWS.forEach(([skill,key,q,right,wrong,why],k)=>{const c=choose(right,wrong,k);
  if(!key){put(skill,`${q} ${c.text}. ${ask(c.n)}`,c.letter,null/* 25 Oct: no picture; it only repeated the question in tiny type */,why,[right,dot(right)]);return;}
  const [src,lines]=P(key),text=lines.join(' ');
  put(skill,`${src}: “${text}” ${q} ${c.text}. ${ask(c.n)}`,c.letter,pass(src,lines),why,[right,dot(right)]);});
}};
